course: dictionary
lesson: 8414
title: One word, many meanings
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: Many words have more than one meaning. The words around them show you which meaning the writer wants. Look at the clues before you choose. «Cutting a corner in a race is cheating too: a runner seen doing it is taken off the results.» The clues are race and results, so a runner here is a person who runs in a race.
explain: The same word can name a thing instead of a person. A sledge slides over snow on runners, which are thin strips that something slides on. Strawberry plants send out runners, long stems that spread along the ground and grow new plants.
explain: Some meanings change with the country you are in. «Her runners were muddy after the cross-country run.» In Australia, New Zealand and some parts of the UK, runners means running shoes. The word cross-country tells you the shoes were worn in a race across the fields.
explain: To pick the right meaning, try each one in the sentence and see which makes sense. If the sentence still feels strange, check a dictionary.
fact: Many words have more than one meaning, so always check the clues around the word.
fact: A runner can be a person who runs in a race, such as a marathon runner.
fact: A runner can also be a thin blade or strip that something slides on, like a sledge or a drawer.
fact: A runner can be a plant stem that spreads along the ground and makes new plants, as strawberry plants do.
fact: In Australia, New Zealand and some parts of the UK, runners means running shoes, also called trainers.
exercise: «A runner seen cutting a corner is taken off the results.» What is a runner in this sentence? | *A person who runs in a race | A long stem that spreads along the ground | A thin blade that a sledge slides on | A kind of bean grown in a garden
exercise: A sledge glides over the snow on its runners. What is a runner here? | A pair of running shoes | *A thin blade or strip that something slides on | A person who races across the snow | A plant that spreads its stems
exercise: Strawberry plants send out runners to make new plants. What is a runner here? | *A long stem that spreads along the ground | A person who runs a race | A runner bean on a pole | A pair of running shoes
test: «Humans are among the best long-distance runners of all animals.» What does runners mean here? | *People or animals that run | A thin blade under a sledge | Running shoes | A plant with long stems along the ground
test: In Australia, Sam says, «My runners are soaking.» What does runners mean? | A thin strip under a drawer | *Running shoes | A plant that spreads by stems | A person in a race
exam: «Her new runners were muddy after the cross-country race.» Which meaning fits best? | A thin blade that something slides on | A plant that spreads along the ground | A runner in the race, like the winner who crossed the line | *Running shoes, as worn in a race
